# Website Generator - AI Development Guidelines
# =============================================
# These rules ensure consistent, high-quality code generation.
#
# ⚠️  IMPORTANT: Always read `docs/DEVELOPMENT.md` first!
#     It contains current state, pending tasks, and context.
#
# 📁 Documentation: See `docs/` folder for full project docs.

## Content & Configuration

- Always read `config/site.ts` for text content (names, links, descriptions). Never hardcode site-specific text in components.
- Use the typed `siteConfig` object for all configurable values.
- When adding new configuration options, extend the TypeScript interfaces in `config/site.ts`.

## Styling & Theming

- Always use Tailwind utility classes referencing CSS variables (e.g., `bg-primary`, `text-foreground`) instead of arbitrary colors (e.g., `bg-blue-500`, `text-gray-700`).
- Define all colors, spacing, and design tokens in `app/globals.css` using CSS variables.
- Follow the HSL color format for all color variables (without the `hsl()` wrapper).
- Use the `cn()` utility from `lib/utils.ts` for conditional class merging.

## Component Organization

- **`components/ui`**: Reserved for atomic UI primitives (Button, Input, Card, etc.). These are building blocks, not features.
- **`components/modules`**: Reserved for "Harvested" reusable features (Hero, Navbar, Footer, BlogGrid, etc.). These must be generic and configurable.
- **`components/custom`**: Reserved for "Vibecoded" client-specific components (PizzaCalculator, CustomWidget, etc.). One-off features that won't be reused.

## Component Guidelines

- When creating generic, reusable features → place in `components/modules`.
- When creating unique, one-off features → place in `components/custom`.
- All components should use TypeScript with proper type definitions.
- Prefer composition over prop drilling for complex components.
- Export both named and default exports for flexibility.

## Icons & Assets

- Use `lucide-react` for all icons. Import individually: `import { IconName } from "lucide-react"`.
- Avoid inline SVGs when a Lucide icon exists.
- Store static assets in the `public/` directory.

## Code Style

- Use TypeScript strict mode. No `any` types without explicit justification.
- Prefer functional components with hooks over class components.
- Use `"use client"` directive only when client-side features are needed.
- Keep server components as the default for better performance.

## File Naming

- Use kebab-case for file names: `hero-section.tsx`, `blog-grid.tsx`.
- Use PascalCase for component names: `HeroSection`, `BlogGrid`.
- Index files (`index.ts`) for clean imports from directories.

## Accessibility

- Include proper ARIA labels on interactive elements.
- Ensure keyboard navigation works correctly.
- Maintain sufficient color contrast (use CSS variables to ensure this).
- Add `focus-ring` utility class to focusable elements.

## Performance

- Use Next.js Image component for all images.
- Lazy load components below the fold.
- Minimize client-side JavaScript; prefer Server Components.
- Use dynamic imports for heavy libraries.

## Feature Flags

- Check `siteConfig.features` before rendering optional functionality.
- Example: `{siteConfig.features.enableBlog && <BlogSection />}`

## Documentation

- **Always read `docs/DEVELOPMENT.md` first** when starting a session.
- Update documentation after significant changes:
  - `docs/DEVELOPMENT.md` - Current state and next steps
  - `docs/CHANGELOG.md` - What was changed
- Key docs:
  - `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` - Project structure
  - `docs/GENERATOR.md` - How generation works
  - `docs/WIZARD.md` - UI documentation

## When Unsure

1. **Read the docs** in `docs/` folder first.
2. Check existing components in `components/modules` for patterns.
3. Refer to `config/site.ts` for configuration structure.
4. Review `app/globals.css` for available CSS variables.
5. Ask for clarification rather than making assumptions.

